the case of Kentucky would say terrible things, in fact, they talked about them being desperately poor, but they would also go on to say things like they were ignorant of the simplest principles of sanitation and hygiene, that they were living in crowded quarters, and they would almost make it seem as if the poor were responsible, in fact, for their own situation, and that they were, in fact for their own situation and that they were in fact causing the very epidemic conditions from which they were reeling. You would imagine it would be difficult for the volunteers to truly play a helping role when they had such attitudes about the people they were trying to help.

